  "id": "COMPOUND:16166 - PROTEIN:5624",
  "verbalization": "Traxillagenin appears to have a shared target of T-cell surface glycoprotein CD3, which is subsequently targeted by different compounds, including Lup-20(29)-en-3-ol, Caragasinin B, and (1R,2R,5S)-(-)-Neomenthyl acetate. All these compounds ultimately target A498, a cell line that is commonly used in cancer research. The evidence suggests that Traxillagenin may act through a common pathway, targeting CD3, which is a key component of the T-cell receptor complex. This mechanism could imply that Traxillagenin may exert its effects by modulating T-cell function, potentially influencing immune responses or cell survival in A498 cells. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible role for Traxillagenin in targeting immune-related processes, with A498 serving as a model for studying such effects. The evidence supports a mechanism where Traxillagenin may interact with CD3, leading to downstream effects on A498 cells, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
